    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born after his father's death at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a> manor, Newton developed his theories on calculus, optics and the law of gravitation at home in the two years after 1665, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> closed against the plague.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a> of 1687 stated three laws of motion and the law of universal gravitation, which formed the dominant scientific viewpoint for centuries until it was superseded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">theory of relativity</a>.</li><li>He spent his last three decades in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> at the Royal Mint and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>, fell out with Hooke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a> and Flamsteed, and kept his rejection of the Trinity and most of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">alchemy</a> unpublished.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Newton's library contained 1,752 books that could be identified, of which 477 were on theology and 169 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">alchemy</a>; books related to his famous scientific work made up slightly less than 12% of the collection.</p></aside>
    </section>

